  <h1>India’s Climate Diplomacy — Balancing Development and Global Responsibility</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>Climate diplomacy is now a central pillar of India’s foreign policy. In 2024, The Hindu reported on India’s stance at COP28 and its efforts to balance climate responsibility with developmental needs. For UPSC, this theme bridges international relations, environment, and economy.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Current Status<br/>•	Net Zero Target: By 2070 (announced at COP26, Glasgow).<br/>•	2030 Targets:<br/>o	500 GW non-fossil capacity.<br/>o	50% electricity from renewables.<br/>o	Reduce emissions intensity by 45% (2005 baseline).<br/>•	Global Standing: 4th largest emitter but per capita emissions ~1/3rd of global average.<br/>•	ISA: International Solar Alliance with >110 member countries.<br/>________________________________________<br/>India’s Diplomatic Approach<br/>1.	Climate Justice: Historical responsibility of developed nations.<br/>2.	CBDR Principle: Common but differentiated responsibilities under UNFCCC.<br/>3.	Finance Demand: Calls for $100 billion annual climate finance delivery.<br/>4.	Technology Transfer: Push for open access to green technologies.<br/>5.	South–South Leadership: Positioning as voice of Global South.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Achievements<br/>•	ISA Success: Global recognition of India-led solar initiative.<br/>•	LiFE Mission: Promoting sustainable lifestyles globally.<br/>•	Green Energy Push: Partnerships with EU, US, Japan on renewables.<br/>•	Credibility: Meeting Paris Agreement commitments ahead of schedule.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Challenges<br/>1.	Energy Security vs Climate Goals: Coal remains 55% of power mix.<br/>2.	Finance Gaps: Actual climate finance far below pledges.<br/>3.	Adaptation Needs: Vulnerability to heatwaves, floods, sea-level rise.<br/>4.	Geopolitical Pressures: Balancing ties with fossil fuel suppliers (Russia, Middle East).<br/>5.	Domestic Constraints: Land conflicts, state–centre tensions on renewable rollouts.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Global Context<br/>•	US & EU: Net zero by 2050, strong green subsidies.<br/>•	China: Net zero by 2060 but massive renewable expansion.<br/>•	India: Advocates fairness while scaling domestic green transition.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Significance<br/>•	Foreign Policy: Climate change as tool of global influence.<br/>•	Economic Transformation: Green economy for jobs and growth.<br/>•	Strategic Autonomy: Balancing energy imports with renewable leadership.<br/>•	Global South Solidarity: India as climate voice for developing nations.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (IR): Climate diplomacy, multilateral institutions.<br/>•	GS-III (Environment): Mitigation, adaptation policies.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Climate justice is intergenerational justice.”<br/>o	“India’s climate diplomacy is about survival and leadership.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: Global environmental governance.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Climate Finance Mobilisation: Push developed nations for commitments.<br/>2.	Technology Partnerships: Joint R&D in hydrogen, storage, carbon capture.<br/>3.	Adaptation Investment: Climate-resilient agriculture, cities, infrastructure.<br/>4.	Green Alliances: Expand ISA, Coalition for Disaster Resilient Infrastructure (CDRI).<br/>5.	Domestic–Global Synergy: Align climate diplomacy with Atmanirbhar Bharat.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>India’s climate diplomacy seeks to reconcile development imperatives with ecological responsibility. By leading on renewables and championing climate justice, India strengthens both its global standing and domestic sustainability. For UPSC aspirants, this issue exemplifies how international relations, environment, and economy converge.</p>
